<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sampot">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sampot</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sompak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sampot is the traditional lower garment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, a cloth wrapped around the body; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> word comes from a root meaning 'to encircle'. It dates back to the Funan era, when third-century Chinese envoys reported that the king ordered the men of Funan to wear cloth wraps, and in the late 13th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan">Zhou Daguan</a> saw the king and ordinary people alike at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> wrapped in cloth from the waist. Its styles include the chong kben, comparable to the dhoti, and pleated forms seen on Angkorian carvings, and the Siamese court bestowed such cloths, as sompak, on its nobles. Royalty and officials wore the chong kben with a formal jacket, and Cambodians still wear it on special occasions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sampot.jpg" alt="Sampot" width="330" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cambodia4kids.org Beth Kanter from Massachusetts, USA · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khmer_Traditional_Dancing.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
